course: ai
lesson: 10
title: Using AI to learn, not to copy
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Mia's dad has a good rule: using AI for homework is like using a calculator. Fine for checking, bad if you never learn your sums.
explain: The bot is patient. Jake asked it to explain long division three different ways until it finally clicked, and Lily learned what a metaphor is and then wrote three of her own. That is learning.
explain: Copying is different: a summary instead of the book misses the best bit, and asking the bot to "write like a 9-year-old so the teacher won't notice" is planning to deceive, which is worse than a mistake.
explain: AI "detectors" exist, but they make mistakes and can wrongly accuse honest pupils. Your best protection is simple: keep your messy notes and drafts, because they show the work is yours.
explain: And remember a teacher can turn the bot into a lesson: find the three mistakes in its history answer, and you become an expert mistake-spotter.
fact: Learn a skill first, then use tools to check it, the way you use a calculator.
fact: A summary leaves things out, and a bot's summary can even get the story wrong.
fact: Making a revision plan with AI is a good use, as long as you then follow the plan yourself.
fact: Teachers often allow AI for brainstorming but not for writing your work; ask first when unsure.
fact: Teachers can often tell when work is not a pupil's own, by style, knowledge and mistakes.
fact: AI detector tools are not always right and can wrongly accuse honest pupils.
fact: Keeping notes and drafts shows that your work is your own.
fact: Planning to hide cheating is worse than making a mistake.
fact: Cheating in a group project can hurt the whole team.
fact: Spotting a bot's mistakes is a real skill that teachers train.
fact: A bot never gets impatient with repeated explanations, but you should still tell your teacher what you found hard.
exercise: Chloe reads the bot's summary instead of the chapter. What goes wrong? | The summary misses the best bit and may get things wrong | Nothing, because summaries always contain everything in the book | The book changes to match the summary | The summary is longer than the book
exercise: Maddie's bot makes a good spelling revision plan. What makes it actually work? | She follows the plan herself | She shows the plan to all her friends | She asks the bot to do the test | She prints the plan in colour
exercise: Jake's teacher says AI is fine for ideas, but he must do the experiment and write it himself. What should Jake do? | Brainstorm with the bot, then do and write it himself | Ask the bot to write the report and then copy it out | Skip the experiment and copy one online | Ignore the teacher and use the bot for all of it
exercise: Mia's friend says teachers can't tell AI work. What is closer to the truth? | Teachers often can tell, though detectors aren't perfect | Teachers can never tell at all, whatever you hand in | Teachers can always tell perfectly | Only head teachers can tell
exercise: Sam is accused of using AI but wrote the essay himself. What should he do? | Calmly show his notes and first draft | Shout that the teacher is a liar and storm out | Admit to cheating so it all ends more quickly | Delete all his notes so no one ever sees them
exercise: Connor asks the bot to "write it like a 9-year-old so the teacher won't notice". Why is this worse? | It is planning to deceive, not a mistake | Bots can't write like 9-year-olds, so it won't work | Teachers prefer older writing | It makes the essay too short
exercise: Lily learns what a metaphor is, then writes three of her own. Is this good? | Yes: learn the idea, then practise it yourself | No, she should copy the bot's metaphors instead | No, metaphors are not allowed in school | Yes, but only if the bot marks them
exercise: One pupil secretly used AI and the whole group was marked down. What does it show? | Cheating can hurt your friends too | Group work is always unfair to someone | Teachers dislike groups | AI makes groups win
exercise: Maddie's class finds three mistakes in a bot's history answer. What skill are they learning? | Spotting a bot's mistakes | Writing history with a bot | Copying answers out faster | Agreeing with everything the bot says
exercise: Jake needs long division explained three times. What else should he do? | Tell his teacher what he found hard | Hide it, because needing help is embarrassing | Stop doing long division | Ask the bot to do all his sums
exercise: Mia's dad compares AI to a calculator. What does he mean? | Fine for checking, bad if you never learn the skill | Calculators and AI are both banned in every school | AI is only for adding numbers | You should never learn sums
exercise: What is the best protection against a wrong AI accusation? | Keeping your notes and drafts | Writing everything in capital letters | Using a detector on your own work | Never handing any work in at all
exercise: Why might an AI detector be unfair? | It can make mistakes and accuse honest pupils | It is always perfectly right about everything | It only checks the spelling | It belongs to the pupils
exercise: When you aren't sure whether AI is allowed, what should you do? | Ask the teacher first | Use it and hope nobody notices | Use it and hide it well | Ask a friend's bot instead
test: "just read the bot summary, the book's way too long" What's the risk? | You miss the best bits, and the summary may be wrong | None, because summaries are perfect copies of the book | The book will be angry | You'll know more than the readers
test: What would happen if Sam had thrown away all his notes and drafts? | It would be harder to show the essay was his | Nothing, because notes and drafts never matter | The teacher would believe him at once | The detector would apologise
test: Your mate brags, "I told the bot to make it sound like a kid, genius." What's your honest view? | That's planning to deceive, worse than a mistake | That's smart, because teachers never learn the trick | That's fine if it's only once | That's what every good pupil does
test: A detector says Lily's own story is "98% AI". What is the sensible thought? | Detectors can be wrong; she can show her drafts | She must have used AI without knowing it | 98% means she copied almost all of it from a bot | She should rewrite it worse on purpose
test: "it's fine to use the bot, it's like a calculator innit" When is that true? | When you already know the skill and use it to check | Always, for everything, right from the very start | Never, calculators are cheating too | Only for spelling tests
test: In a group, Tom used AI secretly. What would have been the honest move? | Telling the team what tools he used | Hiding it better | Using AI for the whole group's work | Blaming another pupil
test: The bot explains long division a fourth way and Jake finally gets it. What next? | Practise some sums himself | Never do any division ever again | Ask the bot for all the answers | Teach the bot how to do division
test: Why does a teacher show the class a bot answer full of mistakes? | To practise checking and spotting errors | To prove that bots are always right about history | To make the class copy it | To show which bot is best
test: "can i use the bot for my science project or what" What did Jake's teacher say? | Yes for ideas, but do and write it yourself | No, never touch it, not even for ideas | Yes, let it do everything | Only if it's a short project
test: A revision plan sits in Maddie's drawer, never used. What happened to its value? | Little; the plan only helps if she follows it | It still makes her learn by itself, just by being there | It works better in a drawer | The bot will do the revising
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate use of a chatbot in respect of homework? | As a means of checking work after the skill itself has been acquired | As a substitute for the acquisition of the skill itself by the pupil | As a means of concealing the absence of effort | As a source of finished work for submission
exam: It is given that a pupil is falsely accused of submitting work generated by a chatbot. Identify the most effective evidence of authorship. | The pupil's notes and successive drafts | The verdict of an automated detection tool | The length of the submitted work | The pupil's declaration made in a raised voice
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning automated tools for the detection of machine-written text. | They are fallible and may wrongly accuse honest pupils | They are infallible in every case | They detect only errors of spelling | They are operated by the pupils themselves
exam: Ascertain the conduct which constitutes the graver fault. | Deliberately disguising the use of a chatbot to deceive the teacher | Making an honest error in a calculation | Requesting a second explanation of a difficult topic | Seeking the teacher's permission before using a tool
